<!--
  Merge into ios/App/App/Info.plist after `npx cap add ios`. Keep this file and
  the checked-in Info.plist in sync — this is the template a regenerated
  project is rebuilt from, so a fix made only in one silently reverts the next
  time someone runs `cap add ios`.

  iOS shows these strings verbatim in the permission prompt, so they are
  written for the person holding the phone, not for the App Store reviewer.
  "Always" is what keeps the clock honest when the screen is off; without it
  iOS only reports while the app is open. The "Always" string says tracking is
  continuous once switched on, not just near a job site — it is, every fix
  from the START TRACKING tap onward is recorded, on the drive to site and
  back included, so the description has to say that rather than imply the
  opposite.
-->
<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
<string>Crewline clocks you in automatically when you arrive at a job site.</string>

<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
<string>Crewline records your location continuously while tracking is switched on — not only near a job site — so your hours keep recording with your phone in your pocket. Choose "Always" so this keeps working with the screen off. Turn tracking off any time to stop.</string>

<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
<array>
  <string>location</string>
</array>

<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
<string>Take site photos and photograph receipts.</string>

<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
<string>Save site photos you take in Crewline.</string>

<!--
  Every network call is HTTPS to Vercel and Supabase, and the only
  cryptography beyond TLS is Supabase's own auth — both are Apple's "exempt"
  standard encryption, and nothing here implements its own cipher. Without
  this, App Store Connect asks on every single upload.
-->
<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
<false/>

<!--
  No UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities entry. "armv7" is a 32-bit-era flag that
  blocks nothing on a modern install — every current device is arm64 — and is
  actively wrong to declare on an arm64-only build: it asserts an instruction
  set the binary does not contain.
-->
